   "response_text": "Woof, all verified. Here's the scoop on httpx redirect handling:\n\n**(1) TooManyRedirects raise line.** The `raise TooManyRedirects(` statement lives at **line 972** of `httpx/_client.py`, inside the sync `_send_handling_redirects` (`if len(history) > self.max_redirects:`). The async client's mirror image is at line 1719 with identical code \u2014 both clients share the same redirect logic. I'm reporting 972 as the (first/sync) occurrence.\n\n**(2) Status codes that can flip the method to GET.** From `_redirect_method`:\n- 303 (SEE_OTHER) \u2192 GET unless HEAD\n- 302 (FOUND) \u2192 GET unless HEAD\n- 301 (MOVED_PERMANENTLY) \u2192 GET only if the method was POST\n\nSo: **301, 302, 303**. (307/308 retain the method, so they're out.)\n\n**(3) Headers stripped when switching to GET.** In `_redirect_headers`, body-only headers get popped: **Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding**.\n\n**(4) Header removed on every redirect, regardless of origin/method.** The `Cookie` header is always popped (`headers.pop(\"Cookie\", None)`) so the client cookie store can re-derive it. Note: `Authorization` is *not* universally removed \u2014 only when crossing origin and not an HTTP\u2192HTTPS upgrade. So the answer is **Cookie**.\n\nOnly read operations were used \u2014 no files modified. \ud83d\udc36\n\nANSWER: 972|301, 302, 303|Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding|Cookie",
